{
  "index": 2,
  "response": "Sure. Here's a summary of the main points:\n\n1. The decision to continue or discontinue sati is surrounded by an awful responsibility.\n\n\n2. To consent to the consignment year after year of hundreds of innocent victims to a cruel and untimely end is a predicament which no conscience can contemplate without horror.\n\n\n3. On the other hand, to put to hazard by a contrary course the very safety of the British Empire in India is an alternative which itself may be considered a still greater evil.\n\n\n4. When we had powerful neighbours and greater reason to doubt our own security, expediency might recommend a more cautious proceeding, but now that we are supreme, my opinion is decidedly in favour of an open and general prohibition.\n\n\n5. William Bentinck, Govenor-General of India, \"On the Suppression of Sati,\" 1829 suggests that the Indians must be ruled with a rod of iron to bring and keep them in a proper state of subordination.\n\n\n6. George Simpson, Head of Northern Department, Hudson's Bay Company, 1826 suggests that the tone of the first passage best supports the supposition that British authorities should let the Indians feel their dependence on the foodstuffs and manufactured goods they sell them.",
